{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 defends the expert on the grounds that checklists are too coarse to capture non-linear interactions, but its own counterconditions concede entire task classes where this defense collapses (highly algorithmic tasks lacking complex non-linear dependencies; experts suffering cognitive decay). The problem asks the arguer to identify evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance tracking right answers. SRC_001 never supplies that evidentiary discriminator; it only asserts that panel 'coverage' is illusory on interaction-dependent outcomes, leaving no within-evaluation test for when checklist success is genuine vs. coincidental. Thus it answers the advocacy half while omitting the distinguishing-evidence half the problem requires.","counterexample":[{"distinguishing_evidence":"Hold out a subset of interaction-dependent items where the checklist's discrete cues remain individually satisfied but the correct outcome flips; if panel accuracy stays high only on additive items and collapses on interaction items, the checklist is tracking additive surface cues, not genuine reliability."}],"target":"3333adb6cca5d86cc66fc8e1d98315a056e1639ac9106d5166458fada1f6d2bc"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's variance-reduction mechanism presupposes independent binary verifications and invokes the Law of Large Numbers to cancel idiosyncratic noise, but its countercondition admits tasks where variables cannot be decomposed without losing emergent information. On exactly those tasks the 'independent sub-claims' assumption fails: non-expert errors on the sub-claims become correlated because they all miss the same interaction, so aggregation no longer cancels noise and can amplify a shared blind spot. SRC_002 gives no within-evaluation evidence that would reveal this correlated-error breakdown versus a genuine reliability gain, so it leaves the problem's distinguishing-evidence requirement unmet.","counterexample":[{"distinguishing_evidence":"Measure inter-rater agreement on each sub-claim and cross-validate with outcome correctness: high agreement on sub-claims plus high accuracy on additive items but systematic failure on items requiring synthetic integration would show the panel is complying with the checklist, not achieving genuine reliability on the interaction-dependent subset."}],"target":"c2de9d8b71a0eaaf25a695bd8c2b487604c9b7601d08b7ed8100a9b396f03eac"}]}